      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{7}{4}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\frac{7}{2},\\,y=-\\frac{7}{4}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +\\frac{9}{2},\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y?$\n\nThe range of $x$ is {{field-1}}{{field-2}}$x\\le ${{field-3}}.\n\nThe range of $y$ is {{field-4}}$\\le y${{field-5}}{{field-6}}.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi,$ we have $-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}\\theta\\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ \\frac{7}{4} & \\text{ }\\ge -\\frac{7}{4}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\text{ }\\ge -\\frac{7}{4} \\\\ -\\frac{7}{4} & \\le -\\frac{7}{4}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\le \\frac{7}{4} \\\\ -\\frac{7}{4}+\\frac{7}{2} & \\le -\\frac{7}{4}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\frac{7}{2}\\le \\frac{7}{4}+\\frac{7}{2} \\\\ \\frac{7}{4} & \\le x\\le \\frac{21}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $\\frac{7}{4}\\le x\\le \\frac{21}{4}.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi,$ we have $-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}\\theta\\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ \\frac{7}{4} & \\text{ }\\ge -\\frac{7}{4}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\text{ }\\ge -\\frac{7}{4} \\\\ -\\frac{7}{4} & \\le -\\frac{7}{4}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\le \\frac{7}{4} \\\\ -\\frac{7}{4}+\\frac{9}{2} & \\le -\\frac{7}{4}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +\\frac{9}{2}\\le \\frac{7}{4}+\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ \\frac{11}{4} & \\le y\\le \\frac{25}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $\\frac{11}{4}\\le y\\le \\frac{25}{4}.$",
